window.products.push({"id":648860,"name":"Electric Butter Churn 2.6 Gallon Capacity with Stainless Steel Body","category":"homeandappliances","retail price":569.0,"sale price":null,"image":"https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/810OO5xq7TL._AC_SL1500_.jpg","description":"Our 2.6 Gallon Electric Butter Churn features a stainless steel dasher (agitator), 304 grade stainless steel body with clip-on lid latches, and a 115 V electric motor. Churn can process 7 quarts of cream at a time. Weighs approx. 14 lbs. Approx. Dimensions: 11\" (diameter) x 14.5\" (height). A butter churn is a simple device that makes it easy to convert the cream from the milk into butter. While using the device, the cream will be churned into a state where the butter and buttermilk are separated. This allows you to acquire the delicious homemade butter! There is actually science behind the butter churn and how butter is made. While the butter churn is working, the cream will be stirred continuously at a high speed. This helps to break down the fat molecules and clumps them all in one place together. At the same time, this process can create air bubbles that will take the liquid and generates foam as one of the substances separates out from the other. This results in buttermilk, which will remain a liquid, and the butter, which is the solids clumped together. To get the cream that you need to create butter, you can use a cream separator or just allow the natural process of separation to occur until the cream rises to the surface. Butter is made out of cow’s milk traditionally. This is often because cow’s milk is more cost effective to do this. It does take a good deal of effort to collect cream out of goat’s milk because the globules are quite a bit smaller. Getting a thick layer of cream to the surface of the goat milk can take a long time. Technically, you can use goat milk to make butter, but it takes more time and you will need more goat's milk to make it happen. Cow’s milk allows this process to be done more efficiently and faster. If you are using an electric butter churn, the ideal temperature to work with is between 55 - 59 degrees F or 13 to 15 degrees C . Room temperature can work as well if you are not able to change it though it may take a bit longer.","images":["https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/810OO5xq7TL._AC_SL1500_.jpg","https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/61zNIJh6ZCL._SL1500_.jpg","https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/61TWUez1VxL._AC_SL1500_.jpg","https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/51TIpnkrEpL._AC_SL1500_.jpg","https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/51I6jTu8yCL._AC_SL1300_.jpg","https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/31o9GyU6jnL._AC_.jpg"],"specifications":{"brand":"Shenandoah Homestead Supply","source_asin":"B0BR8GMD42","category_path":"Home & Kitchen"},"sku":"BM-648860","productType":"Home & Appliances","inventory":0,"age_group":"Adult","gender":"Unisex","source_url":"https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0BR8GMD42","source_catalog":"Amazon Reviews 2023 product metadata","source_file":"meta_Home_and_Kitchen_2023.jsonl","source_asin":"B0BR8GMD42","price_pending":true,"merchant_ready":false});
